BIGGEST GAP: `slag-rs/src/sexp/parser.rs:116-123` keeps backslashes in quoted values. A founder (Gemini did this) wrote `:proof "python3 -c \"import hello; ...\""`; the parser skips past `\"` but keeps the backslash, so the proof runs verbatim with `\"` in it, bash reports a syntax error, the ingot burns 5 heats plus a re-smelt and cracks ($0.59, 7 minutes, nothing forged). Reproduced with a hand-written PLAN.md on another provider: cracked in 16 s, FLUX log `Proof failed [python3 -c \"import hello…\"]: bash: syntax error`. The operator line printed only "the smith's check passed; the ingot's proof or bar did not" (`render/tape.rs:863`), hiding the cause.

Required:
1. Quoted values unescape `\"` and `\\` (and keep any other backslash sequence as written, so regexes and printf formats in proofs survive). The writer that saves ingots back to PLAN.md must escape the same way, so parse(write(x)) == x for any string, including ones with quotes, backslashes, newlines and `$`. Property-style tests with tricky proofs (grep regexes with `\.` and `\|`, printf with `\n`, nested quotes, `python3 -c "..."`).
2. A proof that fails because the shell could not parse it (bash syntax error, command not found, exit 126/127) is a broken proof, not a failed attempt: say so in one readable line naming the proof and the shell's first error line, and do not burn heats re-running the smith against a proof that can never pass (route it to re-smelt to rewrite the proof, once).
3. The operator line at `render/tape.rs:863` (or wherever it now lives) shows the proof's actual failure reason (first line of its stderr, clipped) instead of the generic sentence. Minimal change there.

